\section*{Constant-factor hardness of directed feedback vertex set}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 102; 2026-09-23), the asserted result is that directed feedback vertex set is NP-hard to approximate within any prescribed constant, even without weights.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 102; 2026-09-23), rank and wildcard vertices encode a collection of ordering tests. A low-cost deletion set leaves a topological order whose pivotal-label budget and shared-prefix conditioning decode an outer-game strategy; unweighted realization preserves the gap.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 102; 2026-09-23).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 102; 2026-09-23), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: directed feedback vertex set is NP-hard to approximate within any prescribed constant, even without weights.

The selected statement includes the clause used for this problem.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{DirectedFeedback.lean}, \texttt{MinUncut.lean}, \texttt{OptimalMaxCut.lean}, \texttt{UniqueGamesTheorem.lean}, \texttt{VertexCover.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.DirectedFeedback.main}, \texttt{OAI.MinUncut.main}, \texttt{OAI.OptimalMaxCut.main}, \texttt{OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.theorem11}, \texttt{OAI.VertexCover.every\_fixed\_factor\_below\_two}, \texttt{OAI.VertexCover.explicit\_cover\_gap}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

